Symbols in parentheses are either [[allophone]]s, or found only in [[loanword]]s. The sound {{IPA|/z/}} generally occurs in loanwords, e.g. ''sŵ'' {{IPA|/zuː/}} ('zoo'), although this is usually realised as {{IPA|/s/}} in [[Welsh language#Dialects|northern accents]], e.g. {{IPA|/suː/}}.  The postalveolar affricates {{IPA|/tʃ/}} and {{IPA|/dʒ/}} occur mainly in loanwords, e.g. {{lang|cy|tsips}} {{IPA|/tʃɪps/}} ('chips')  and {{lang|cy|jeli}} {{IPA|/ˈdʒɛli/}} ('[[gelatin dessert|jelly]]'), but also in some dialects as developments from {{IPA|/tj/}} and {{IPA|/dj/}}, e.g. {{IPA|/dʒaul/}} from {{lang|cy|diafol}} {{IPA|/ˈdjavɔl/}} ('devil'). The [[Nasal consonant#Voiceless nasals|voiceless nasals]] {{IPA|/m̥ n̥ ŋ̊/}} occur mostly word-initially, as a consequence of [[Welsh morphology#Nasal mutation|nasal mutation]]. These nasals have recently been interpreted as sequences of {{IPA|/m n ŋ/}} + {{IPA|/h/}} and are often pronounced as such.{{sfnp|Hammond|2019|}}{{sfnp|Bell et al.|2023|p=7}} Initial {{IPA|/χw/}} is colloquially realised as {{IPA|[ʍ]}} in the south, e.g. {{lang|cy|chwech}} {{IPA|/χweːχ/}} ('six') pronounced {{IPA|[ʍeːχ]}}.

===Fortis and lenis===
Some linguists argue that Welsh consonants are better described in terms of [[fortis and lenis]] rather than [[voice (phonetics)|voiced]] and [[voicelessness|unvoiced]]. According to these scholars, not only {{IPA|/p/}}~{{IPA|/b/}} and {{IPA|/t/}}~{{IPA|/d/}} but possibly also {{IPA|/s/}}~{{IPA|/h/}} and {{IPA|/m/}}~{{IPA|/v/}} can be described as fortis–lenis pairs.{{sfnp|Asmus|Jaworski|Baran|2020|pp=5-16}} The fortis consonants {{IPA|/f θ χ p t k ɬ/}} and {{IPA|/s/}}, when they end a monosyllable such as {{lang|cy|map}} 'map' or {{lang|cy|bach}} 'little' or {{lang|cy|hyll}} 'ugly', tend to have a longer duration than the other consonants. Voiceless {{IPA|/r̥/}} does not occur at the end of a word, but is considered to belong to the fortis group. {{IPA|/h/}} despite being partially unvoiced is considered lenis.

===Stops===

The stops {{IPA|/p t k/}} are distinguished from {{IPA|/b d ɡ/}} by means of [[aspiration (phonetics)|aspiration]] more consistently than by [[voicing (phonetics)|voicing]], as {{IPA|/b d ɡ/}} are actually devoiced in some contexts. This devoiced nature is recognised in the spelling of {{IPA|/sp sk/}} as {{angbr|sb sg}}, although {{IPA|/st/}} is [[Orthography|orthographically]] {{angbr|st}} for historical reasons.  In words where {{IPA|/p t k/}} are preceded by a vowel, phonetic studies have shown that at least in some dialects of North and West Wales these consonants are not only followed by aspiration, but also preceded by a period of aspiration. The aspiration before the consonant is shorter than the aspiration that follows it.{{sfnp|Morris|Hejná|2020}}

A study of mainly North Welsh speakers showed that the fortis plosives {{IPA|/p t k/}} are about twice as long as the lenis {{IPA|/b d ɡ/}}, whether in word-initial or in word-final position.{{sfnp|Baran|2020|p=55}} 

When {{IPA|/k/}} and {{IPA|/ɡ/}} are followed by {{IPA|/i(ː)/}} or {{IPA|/e(ː)/}}, as in {{lang|cy|ci}} 'dog' or {{lang|cy|ceg}} 'mouth' they are slightly [[Palatalization (phonetics)|palatalised]]. In some dialects (such as that of [[Bangor, Gwynedd|Bangor]] district) a palatalised {{IPA|[kʲ]}} and {{IPA|[ɡʲ]}} can also be heard before {{IPA|/a(:)/}}, but only in certain loanwords borrowed from English{{sfnp|Fynes-Clinton|1913|p=xxiv}}{{sfnp|Czerniak|2015b|pp=141–2}} The palatalisation is sometimes reflected in spelling, e.g. {{lang|cy|giât}} or {{lang|cy|gât}} 'gate'.<ref>Both spellings are given by the dictionary [https://geiriadur.ac.uk/gpc/gpc.html Geiriadur Prifysgol Cymru].</ref>

===Fricatives===
The fortis fricatives {{IPA|/f, s, θ/}} are slightly longer and have less voicing than the lenis fricatives {{IPA|/v, h, ð/}}. The fricative {{IPA|/h/}} was shown in a phonetic study to have more voicing than {{IPA|/s/}} but less than {{IPA|/v/}}.{{sfnp|Baran|2020|p=57}} {{IPA|/h/}} is usually found only word-initially or at the beginning of a stressed syllable; after a stress it generally disappears, e.g. {{lang|cy|brenhínes}} 'queen' but {{lang|cy|brénin}} 'king'. Consequently {{IPA|/h/}} is not found finally in any word. In parts of south-east Wales, {{IPA|/h/}} disappears in all positions, and in these same dialects {{IPA|/r̥/}} is replaced by {{IPA|/r/}}.{{sfnp|Awbery|2009|p=369}}

The fricatives {{IPA|/v ð/}} are sometimes not pronounced at the end of a word, e.g. {{lang|cy|nesaf}} {{IPA|/nɛsav/}} ('next') realised as {{IPA|/ˈnɛsa/}} or {{lang|cy|i fyny}} {{IPA|/iː ˈvənɨ/}} ('up') from {{lang|cy|mynydd}} {{IPA|/'mənɨð, 'mənɪð/}} ('mountain'). 

The phoneme {{IPA|/ʃ/}} occurs for all speakers in certain words, almost all borrowings (e.g., {{lang|cy|siarad}} 'talk', {{lang|cy|sisial}} 'whisper', {{lang|cy|siop}} 'shop', {{lang|cy|siampŵ}} 'shampoo'). Otherwise its distribution varies regionally. In northern accents, it can occur when {{IPA|/s/}} precedes {{IPA|/iː j/}}, even (depending on region) across some word boundaries, e.g., {{lang|cy|es i}} {{IPA|[ˈeːʃ i]}} ('I went'). In some [[Welsh language#Dialects|southern accents]] it is produced when {{IPA|/s/}} follows {{IPA|/ɪ/}} or {{IPA|/iː/}}, e.g. {{lang|cy|mis}} {{IPA|[miːʃ]}} ('month'). 

The voiceless fricative {{IPA|/χ/}} is realised as uvular except by some southwestern speakers, who produce a velar variant {{IPA|[x]}}.

===Other consonants===

The {{IPA|/r/}} phoneme is pronounced as the [[voiced uvular fricative]] {{IPA|[ʁ]}} by some speakers in [[Dyfed]] and [[Gwynedd]], in a pronunciation known as {{lang|cy|tafod tew}} ('thick tongue').{{sfnp|Wells|1982|p=390}}

In some dialects of north-western Welsh, the {{IPA|/l/}} phoneme is consistently [[dental, alveolar and postalveolar lateral approximants#Dark L|velarised]] or "dark" ({{IPA|[ɫ]}}, not to be confused with {{IPA|[ɬ]}}) in all positions, but remains unvelarised or "clear" ({{IPA|[l]}}) in the south,{{sfnp|Bell et al.|2023|p=494}} except in rare exceptions where {{IPA|[ɫ]}} is found after {{IPA|/d/}}, e.g. {{lang|cy|dlos}} {{IPA|[dɫoːs]}} 'pretty'.

In some words such as {{lang|cy|gwlad}} 'country', {{lang|cy|gwlân}} 'wool', {{lang|cy|gwneud}} '(to) do', {{lang|cy|gwraig}} and their mutated forms {{lang|cy|wlad}}, {{lang|cy|wlân}}, {{lang|cy|wneud}}, {{lang|cy|wraig}}, the letter {{angbr|w}} is not sounded as a vowel but is a consonant, realised by a rounding of the lips accompanying the {{IPA|/ɡ/}} or {{IPA|/l, n, r/}}.{{sfnp|Fynes-Clinton|1913|p=xxiv}} These words are considered to be of one syllable only.{{sfnp|Griffiths|Jones|1995|at=Consonant}} As is usual with words beginning with a consonant, the definite article has the form {{lang|cy|y}}, for example {{lang|cy|y wraig}} 'the woman'.

The voiceless sounds {{IPA|/m̥, n̥, ŋ̊, r̥/}} are generally "followed by a full, distinctly enunciated {{IPA|/h/}}".{{sfnp|Fynes-Clinton|1913|p=xxiv}} Thus {{lang|cy|fy mhen}} 'my head' and {{lang|cy|yng Nghaerdydd}} 'in Cardiff' are pronounced as {{IPA|[ɘ ˈm̥hɛn, ɘŋ ŋ̊haɨ̯rˈdɨːð]}}. Phrases like {{lang|cy|fy mhres}} 'my money' and {{lang|cy|fy nghrys}} 'my shirt', which are spelled with ''hr'', are pronounced with the {{IPA|/h/}} following the {{IPA|/r/}}: {{IPA|[ɘ ˈm̥r̥heːs, ɘ ˈŋ̊r̥hɨːs]}}.{{sfnp|Fynes-Clinton|1913|p=xxiv}}

When a word traditionally ends with a stop or fricative + {{IPA|/l/}} or {{IPA|/r/}}, for example {{lang|cy|pobl}} 'people' or {{lang|cy|ffenestr}} 'window', there is a tendency to adapt it in spoken Welsh to remove the cluster.{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|pp=88–93}} In some cases an [[epenthesis|epenthetic]] vowel, repeating the existing vowel, is added to make a two-syllable word: {{lang|cy|pobl}} > {{IPA|[ˈpɔbɔl]}}, {{lang|cy|cancr}} 'cancer' > {{IPA|[ˈkankar]}}, {{lang|cy|brwydr}} 'battle' > {{IPA|[ˈbruɨdɨr]}}. If this strategy would result in an antepenultimate accent, the {{IPA|/l/}} or {{IPA|/r/}} may simply be deleted: {{lang|cy|ffenestr}} 'window' > {{IPA|[ˈfɛnɛst]}}, {{lang|cy|posibl}} 'possible' > {{IPA|['pɔsib]}}. Occasionally, with {{IPA|/θr/}}, there may be [[metathesis (linguistics)|metathesis]]: {{lang|cy|ewythr}} 'uncle' > {{IPA|[ˈewɨrθ]}}. Words like {{lang|cy|marw}} {{IPA|[ˈmarʊ]}} 'dead', {{lang|cy|chwerw}} {{IPA|[ˈχwerʊ]}} 'bitter', {{lang|cy|hoyw}} {{IPA|[ˈhɔjʊ]}} 'gay', which were formerly monosyllabic, are pronounced in modern Welsh with two syllables and the consonant {{IPA|[w]}} is vocalised as the vowel {{IPA|[ʊ]}}; but in {{lang|cy|marwdy}} 'mortuary' and {{lang|cy|chwerwder}} 'bitterness' the {{IPA|[w]}} is usually silent.{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=17–8}} When a word like {{lang|cy|pelydr}} 'ray of light' or {{lang|cy|anadl}} 'breath' is pronounced colloquially with three syllables, the accent remains unchanged on the first syllable.

===Geminated consonants===
Certain consonants in Welsh can be pronounced lengthened or [[gemination|geminated]], even though this is not reflected in the spelling. This gemination only occurs after a strongly stressed vowel: thus the {{IPA|/t/}} is long in {{lang|cy|áteb}} {{IPA|[ˈatːʰɛb]}} 'answer' but short in {{lang|cy|atébion}} {{IPA|[aˈtʰɛbjɔn]}} 'answers', where the preceding vowel is unstressed.{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|p=21}} In many cases, geminated consonants are derived historically from a merging of two separate consonants.

According to Morris-Jones (1921), the consonants {{IPA|/p, t, k, s, ɬ, m, ŋ/}} are double, and shorten the preceding vowel; he adds that {{IPA|/n/}} and {{IPA|/r/}} are also double in some words where they are written with {{angbr|nn}} and {{angbr|rr}}, and in a few words {{IPA|/l/|}} is double (although not shown in spelling).{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=27}} To these consonants Fynes-Clinton (1913) adds {{IPA|/f, θ, χ/}}. However, in modern Welsh, not only these consonants but also other consonants such as {{IPA|/b, d, ɡ/}} are also sometimes pronounced lengthened after a stressed vowel. The distinction between long and short {{IPA|/n, r, l/}} mentioned by Morris-Jones appears to be generally no longer made, so that {{lang|cy|tonnau}} 'waves' and {{lang|cy|tonau}} 'tones' are usually pronounced identically, except for the half-long vowel of the latter in southern dialects.{{sfnp|Williams|1983|pp=56}} 

In her thesis on the perception of stress, Williams notes that if the words {{lang|cy|ymladd}} {{IPA|/ˈəmlað/}} 'to fight' and {{lang|cy|ymlâdd}} {{IPA|/əmˈlaːð/}} 'to tire oneself out' are compared, the length of the {{IPA|/m/}} in the former, following a stressed vowel, is longer than the {{IPA|/m/}} in the latter, where it follows an unstressed vowel.{{sfnp|Williams|1983|pp=53}}

The shortening of a double consonant to a single one after an unstressed vowel can be heard even when the consonants are in different words. Thus {{lang|cy|Port Talbot}} 'Port Talbot' is pronounced {{IPA|[pɔrˈtalbɔt]}}, and {{lang|cy|iechyd da}} 'good health!' is pronounced {{IPA|[jaχɪˈdaː]}}. In phrases with nasal mutation of a voiced plosive, like {{lang|cy|ym Mangor}} 'in Bangor', the {{IPA|/m/}} can be pronounced either single or double: {{IPA|[ǝm(m)áŋgɔr]}}.{{sfnp|Hammond|2019|pp=38}}

This lengthening is found not only in Welsh but also in the English of speakers with a Welsh accent, especially of bilingual speakers.{{sfnp|Collée|2016|pp=13–19, 21}} According to J.C. Wells (1982), the phenomenon is most obvious in voiceless consonants such as {{IPA|/p, t, θ, s/}} in words such as ''chapel'', ''matter'', ''nothing'', ''missing'', but can also sometimes be heard in words such as ''ready'', ''ever'' with {{IPA|/d, v/}} following a stressed penultimate. However, the phenomenon seems not to occur in some parts of South Wales such as Gwent and Cardiff.{{sfnp|Wells|1982|pp=387–8}}

===Consonant mutations===
{{further|Literary Welsh morphology#Initial consonant mutation}}
An important feature of Welsh (as of other [[Celtic languages]]) is the system of consonant mutations, in which certain consonants are replaced by other consonants in certain contexts. These mutations are found both at the beginning of words, and internally in compound words. Three different types of mutation are recognised: the soft mutation; the nasal mutation; and the aspirate mutation. For example, {{lang|cy|tad}} {{IPA|/taːd/}} 'father' (the radical form); {{lang|cy|ei dad}} {{IPA|/i daːd/}} 'his father' (soft mutation); {{lang|cy|(fy) nhad}} {{IPA|/və n̥aːd/}} 'my father' (nasal mutation); {{lang|cy|ei thad}} {{IPA|/i θaːd/}} 'her father' (aspirate mutation).{{sfnp|King|2003|pp=13–19}}

Some mutations have a grammatical function (for example, distinguishing masculine from feminine, or subject from object, or noun from adverb). Other mutations occur automatically, for example after words such as {{lang|cy|o}} 'from' (followed by a soft mutation), {{lang|cy|yn}} 'in' (followed by a nasal mutation) or {{lang|cy|a}} 'and' (followed by an aspirate mutation), or when a noun is preceded by an adjective or prefix, e.g. {{lang|cy|hen ddyn}} {{IPA|/heːn ðɨːn/}} 'old man' (soft mutation). The presence of the definite article blocks a mutation caused by a preceding word, e.g. {{lang|cy|o ddrws}} {{IPA|/ɔ ðruːs/}} 'from a door' (soft mutation), but {{lang|cy|o'r drws}} {{IPA|/ɔr druːs/}} 'from the door' (no mutation).{{sfnp|King|2003|pp=13–19}} The changes which occur in the soft mutation derive historically from [[lenition]] of consonants following a vowel. However, in many cases the vowel which caused the lenition is no longer pronounced, so in modern Welsh the soft mutation exists independently, occurring for example after the article {{lang|cy|y}} 'the' in a feminine singular noun, but not in a masculine one.

* In the soft mutation, which is the most common, {{IPA|/p, t, k, b, d, ɡ, m, r̥, ɬ/}} mutate to {{IPA|/b, d, ɡ, v, ð, ∅, v, r, l/}}. In colloquial Welsh, {{IPA|/tʃ/}} can mutate to {{IPA|/dʒ/}}. In certain circumstances, for example when a feminine singular noun follows the definite article, the other seven consonants mutate, but {{IPA|/ɬ/}} and {{IPA|/r̥/}} do not mutate; this is called limited soft mutation. The soft mutation of {{IPA|/ɡ/}} is silent in most words, but in some borrowings from English, such as {{lang|cy|gêm}} {{IPA|/ɡeːm/}} 'game', {{IPA|/ɡ/}} does not mutate.

* In the nasal mutation, {{IPA|/p, t, k, b, d, ɡ/}} mutate into {{IPA|/m̥, n̥, ŋ̊, m, n, ŋ/}}. In colloquial Welsh, {{IPA|/m/}} and {{IPA|/n/}} can sometimes mutate to {{IPA|/m̥/}} and {{IPA|/n̥/}}. Other consonants are unaffected.

* In the aspirate mutation, {{IPA|/p, t, k/}} mutate to {{IPA|/f, θ, χ/}}. Other consonants are unaffected, or in some circumstances may undergo the soft mutation.

* The fricative consonants {{IPA|/f, h, s, ʃ, χ, v, θ, ð/}} and the approximants {{IPA|/w, j/}} never mutate.

A contrary process called [[provection]], whereby {{IPA|/b, d, ɡ, v, ð/}} are hardened to {{IPA|/p, t, k, f, θ/}}, also exists in Welsh. One circumstance where this occurs is when a word has been extended by a suffix which historically began with {{IPA|/h/}}: the {{IPA|/h/}} disappears in post-stress position, hardening and doubling the consonant to which it is joined, e.g. {{lang|cy|bwyd}} + ''-ha'' > {{lang|cy|bwyta}} {{IPA|[bʊɨtːa]}} 'eating'; {{lang|cy|gwlyb}} 'wet' + ''-hach'' > {{lang|cy|glypach}} {{IPA|[ɡlɘpːaχ]}} 'wettest'; {{lang|cy|atgoffa}} {{IPA|[atˈgɔfːa]}} 'remind', from {{lang|cy|cof}} {{IPA|[koːv]}} 'mind'.

Provection can also occur in compound words where {{IPA|/b, d, ɡ/}} meet {{IPA|/p, t, k/}} (or their soft mutations {{IPA|/b, d, ɡ/}}). Thus {{lang|cy|pob}} + {{lang|cy|peth}} > {{lang|cy|popeth}} {{IPA|[ˈpɔpːɛθ]}} 'everything'; {{lang|cy|bwyd}} + {{lang|cy|tŷ}} > {{lang|cy|bwyty}} {{IPA|[ˈbʊɨtːɨ]}} 'restaurant'.{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|pp=150–4}}

