The vowels {{IPA|/ɨ̞/}} and {{IPA|/ɨː/}} merged with {{IPA|/ɪ/}} and {{IPA|/iː/}} in southern dialects, but are retained in northern dialects. An acoustic study of ten speakers from North Wales showed that some speakers did not distinguish the short vowels {{IPA|/ɪ/}} and {{IPA|/ɨ̞/}}, although all clearly distinguished the long vowels {{IPA|/ɨː/}} and {{IPA|/iː/}}.{{sfnp|Mayr|Davies|2011}} The same study showed that long vowels clearly have longer duration than short ones both in Southern and Northern Welsh.

The length contrast for most vowels in most cases involves also a contrast in vowel quality (e.g., {{IPA|/iː/}} vs. {{IPA|/ɪ/}}) and there is debate as to which is the primary contrasting feature.{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|pp=21–22}} The long counterpart to short {{IPA|/a/}} is sometimes misleadingly transcribed {{IPA|/ɑ/}}. This is often found in solely quality-distinctive transcriptions to avoid using a length mark. The actual pronunciation of long {{IPA|/a/}} is {{IPA|[aː]}}, which makes the vowel pair unique in that for most speakers there is no significant quality difference.{{sfnp|Mayr|Davies|2011}} Regional realisations of {{IPA|/aː/}} may be {{IPA|[æː]}} or {{IPA|[ɛː]}} in north-central and (decreasingly) south-eastern [[Wales]] or sporadically as {{IPA|[ɑː]}} in some southern areas undoubtedly under the influence of English.{{sfnp|Wmffre|2013|p=3}}

Long vowels are found only in stressed syllables, and in North Wales only in stressed final syllables or monosyllables.  Since stress in Welsh is, with a few exceptions, on the penultimate syllable of polysyllabic words, this means that length contrasts mostly occur only in monosyllabic words, apart from a few words such as {{lang|cy|Caerdydd}} 'Cardiff' which are stressed on the final syllable.{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|pp=21–22}} To a large extent the length of the vowel in a stressed final syllable can be predicted by the consonant which follows it. Where there is no final consonant, as in {{lang|cy|tŷ}} 'house', or if the vowel in a final syllable is followed by ''b, d, g, v, dd, ff, th, ch, s'', e.g. {{lang|cy|dydd}} 'day', {{lang|cy|cath}} 'cat', {{lang|cy|bach}} 'small', {{lang|cy|mis}} 'month', the vowel is usually long. When the final syllable ends in ''p, t, c, m, ng, sh, j'' or when it ends with two consonants the vowel is usually short, e.g. {{lang|cy|trwm}} 'heavy', {{lang|cy|twp}} 'stupid', {{lang|cy|llong}} 'ship', {{lang|cy|plant}} 'children', {{lang|cy|corff}} 'body'.{{sfnp|Griffiths|Jones|1995|at=Quantity of vowels}}

Sometimes there are differences between North and South Wales. Words in which the final vowel is followed by ''ll'' or ''s'' before a stop consonant, such as {{lang|cy|gwallt}} 'hair', {{lang|cy|Pasg}} 'Easter', and {{lang|cy|cosb}} 'punishment', tend to have a long vowel in the North, short in the South. Conversely, {{lang|cy|heb}} 'without', and monosyllables ending in ''ll'' such as {{lang|cy|llall}} 'the other', and {{lang|cy|pell}} 'far' tend to have a short vowel in the North, but long in the South. But {{lang|cy|(h)oll}} 'all' has a long vowel even in the North.{{sfnp|Griffiths|Jones|1995|at=Quantity of vowels}}

When a final vowel is followed by ''l'', ''n'', ''r'', the vowel is long in some words, short in others: for example, it is long in {{lang|cy|mil}} 'a thousand', {{lang|cy|hen}} 'old', {{lang|cy|tir}} 'land', but short in {{lang|cy|tal}} 'tall', {{lang|cy|pren}} 'wood', {{lang|cy|car}} 'car'.

Since a vowel can be long only in stressed syllables, in a phrase like {{lang|cy|dros nos}} {{IPA|/drɔs ˈnoːs/}} 'over night', the vowel of the preposition {{lang|cy|dros}} is short, being unstressed, but the vowel of {{lang|cy|nos}} 'night' is long.

The vowel {{IPA|/ə/}} does not occur in the final syllable of words (except a few monosyllabic [[proclitic]]s) and some English words. It is always pronounced short except when emphasised in the name of the letter {{lang|cy|y}}.{{sfnp|Wmffre|2013|p=3}}

In penultimate syllables in North Wales accents all vowels are short. Thus {{lang|cy|cath}} 'cat' has a long vowel, but {{lang|cy|cathod}} 'cats' has a short one. In South Wales in some words, such as {{lang|cy|defaid}} 'sheep (pl)', {{lang|cy|efail}} 'forge', {{lang|cy|ola(f)}} 'last', {{lang|cy|rhedeg}} 'to run', {{lang|cy|ugain}} 'twenty', {{lang|cy|ifanc}} 'young', can be heard with a vowel which has been described as "half-long". Words such as {{lang|cy|lleol}} 'local', where the penultimate vowel is followed by no consonant, also have a half long vowel. In South Wales {{lang|cy|tonau}} {{IPA|cy|ˈtʰoˑnɛ|}} 'tunes' and {{lang|cy|tonnau}} {{IPA|cy|ˈtʰɔnɛ|}} 'waves' are often pronounced differently, but in North Wales identically.{{sfnp|Iosad|2017}}

Vowels in penultimate syllables followed by two consonants, as in {{lang|cy|cynta(f)}} 'first' or {{lang|cy|gorffen}} 'to finish', are usually short, as are vowels followed by a consonant + the semivowel {{IPA|[i̯]}}, as in {{lang|cy|dynion}} 'men' or {{lang|cy|cofio}} 'to remember'. Penultimate vowels before {{IPA|/p/, /t/, /c/, /m/, /ŋ/, /ɫ/, /s/, /ʃ/, /dʒ/}} are usually short{{sfnp|Griffiths|Jones|1995|at=Quantity of vowels}} and the consonants themselves, especially {{IPA|/p/, /t/, /c/, /ɫ/,}} are often pronounced long when following a stressed vowel, e.g. {{lang|cy|hapus}} {{IPA|[ˈhapːʰɨs]}} 'happy', {{lang|cy|capel}} {{IPA|[ˈkapːʰɛl]}} 'chapel', {{lang|cy|ateb}} {{IPA|[ˈatːʰɛb]}} 'to answer', {{lang|cy|tocyn}} {{IPA|[ˈtɔkːʰɨn]}} 'ticket', {{lang|cy|cyllell}} {{IPA|/ˈkəɬːɛɬ/}} 'knife'.{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|pp=21|at=§2.2.6}} The fricative {{IPA|/s/}} has a contradictory effect, making a final stressed vowel long, but a penultimate stressed vowel short; thus {{lang|cy|bys}} {{IPA|[bɨːs]}} 'finger' has a long vowel, but {{lang|cy|bysedd}} {{IPA|[ˈbəsːeð]}} 'fingers' has a short one.

The long vowels are not all derived from [[Proto-Celtic language|Proto-Celtic]] long vowels, which in some cases were transformed into diphthongs in Welsh, but instead from the [[New Quantity System]].{{sfnp|Willis|2009}}

===Diphthongs===
Northern Welsh has at least 13 diphthongs,<ref>Some writers claim more than 13 diphthongs: see {{harvcoltxt|Czerniak|2015a|p=18–9}}. {{harvcoltxt|Fynes-Clinton|1913|p=xiv}} counts up to 24 diphthongs for the Bangor dialect.</ref> but these are collapsed to just 8 diphthongs in Southern Welsh. Thus, Northern Welsh {{lang|cy|(h)ail}} 'second' {{IPA|cy|hai̯l|}}, {{lang|cy|hael}} 'liberal' {{IPA|cy|haːɨ̯l|}}, and {{lang|cy|haul}} 'sun' {{IPA|cy|haɨ̯l|}} are homophones in Southern Welsh and pronounced {{IPA|cy|hai̯l|}}.{{sfnp|Mayr|Davies|2011}}

