The diphthongs containing {{IPA|/ɨ/}} occur only in northern dialects; in southern dialects {{IPA|/ʊɨ/}} is replaced by {{IPA|/ʊi/}} and {{IPA|/ɨu, əɨ~ɛɨ, ɔɨ, a(ː)ɨ/}} are merged with {{IPA|/ɪu, əi~ɛi, ɔi, ai/}}. There is a general tendency in the South to simplify diphthongs in [[Colloquialism|everyday speech]], e.g. Northern {{IPA|/ɡwaːɨθ/}} corresponding to {{IPA|/ɡwaːθ/}} in the South, or Northern {{IPA|/ɡwɛiθjɔ/}} and Southern {{IPA|/ɡwiθɔ/}}. Since different speakers pronounce the diphthongs in different ways, the phonetic transcription can only be approximate. Mayr and Davies note that although {{IPA|/ɔi/}} and {{IPA|/ɔɨ/}} are both usually transcribed with {{IPA|/ɔ/}} as their first element, in fact the two diphthongs start from different vowels.{{sfnp|Mayr|Davies|2011}}{{page needed|date=June 2026}}{{clarify|date=June 2026}}<!--What is the phonetic nature of the difference?-->

For the north Welsh diphthongs ending in {{IPA|/ɨ/}} etc., Hammond (2019) writes {{IPA|/eɰ/, /aɰ/}} etc., noting that the glide in these diphthongs is distinct from the vowel {{IPA|/ɨ/}}.{{sfnp|Hammond|2019|pp=58}}{{clarify|date=June 2026}}<!--What is the phonetic nature of the difference?-->

Some writers classify the diphthongs as long and short. In their acoustic study of 20 different speakers from North and South Wales, {{harvtxt|Mayr|Davies|2011}} found that {{IPA|/ɛi/}} tended to be shorter than other diphthongs in overall length; however, they judged that there was no clear way of dividing the diphthongs into long and short as there is with monophthongs.{{sfnp|Mayr|Davies|2011}} On the other hand, {{harvtxt|Ball|1983}}, testing a speaker from Anglesey pronouncing words containing {{IPA|/ai/, /aɨ/,}} and {{IPA|/aːɨ/}} (for example {{lang|cy|hail}} 'feast', {{lang|cy|haul}} 'sun', {{lang|cy|hael}} 'generous'), found that {{IPA|/aːɨ/}} was not only longer overall than the other two by about 30%, but that the first element in {{IPA|/aːɨ/}} was approximately twice as long as the first element of the other two.{{sfnp|Ball|1983}} Similar spectrographic evidence showed that the first element in {{IPA|/ɔɨ/}} {{angbr|oe}} in the pronunciation of one Northern Welsh speaker was longer than the first element of {{IPA|/ɔi/}} {{angbr|oi}}.{{sfnp|Mayr|Davies|2009}} According to {{harvtxt|Morris-Jones|1921}}, the diphthong {{IPA|/ʊɨ/}} also has a long first element in some words, such as {{lang|cy|hwyr}} 'late', {{lang|cy|mwy}} 'more', {{lang|cy|llwyd}} 'grey', though a short one in others such as {{lang|cy|pwynt}} 'point' and {{lang|cy|rhwystr}} 'obstacle'. He notes that in North Wales there is also a long first element in words such as {{lang|cy|llaw}} 'hand' and {{lang|cy|tew}} 'fat' where there is no final consonant. According to the same author the first element is also long in certain other words such as {{lang|cy|paun}} 'peacock', {{lang|cy|trôi}} 'he was turning'.{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=25–6}} In penultimate syllables, the first element of all diphthongs is short.{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=29}}

Welsh also has rising diphthongs and triphthongs, starting with the glides {{IPA|/w/}} and {{IPA|/j/}}, e.g. {{lang|cy|chwech}} 'six', {{lang|cy|iaith}} 'language'. The glide {{IPA|/w/}} when derived by mutation from {{IPA|/ɡw/}} is usually treated as a consonant, taking the article {{lang|cy|y}}, e.g. {{lang|cy|y wasg}} 'the press'; whereas {{IPA|[j]}} is usually treated as a vowel with the article {{lang|cy|yr}}, e.g. {{lang|cy|yr iaith}} 'the language'. Both glides tend to become devoiced following a voiceless consonant, for example in {{lang|cy|chwerthin}} 'to laugh', {{lang|cy|clociau}} 'clocks'.{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|p=20|at=§2.2.6}}

The digraph {{angbr|wy}} is ambiguous, since it can represent either a falling diphthong ({{IPA|/ʊɨ/, /ʊi/, /uːi/, /uːɨ/}}) or a rising one ({{IPA|/wi/, /wɨ/, /wiː/, /wɨː/}}): falling in {{lang|cy|wythnos}} 'week', {{lang|cy|gwyliau}} 'holidays', {{lang|cy|posibilrwydd}} 'possibility'; rising in {{lang|cy|gwydr}} 'glass', {{lang|cy|digwydd}} 'to happen', {{lang|cy|ffermwyr}} 'farmers'. There are several words which formerly had a falling diphthong but which now are often pronounced with a rising one, especially in North Wales, for example {{lang|cy|wyneb}} 'face', {{lang|cy|ofnadwy}} 'dreadful', {{lang|cy|Conwy}} 'Conwy'.{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=8–12}} In formal Welsh, the definite article remains {{lang|cy|yr}} as usual for words starting with a vowel: {{lang|cy|yr wyneb}} 'the face', {{lang|cy|yr Wyddfa}} 'Mount Snowdon'.

===Vowel mutations===
====Mutations before a suffix====
Vowels and diphthongs in the final syllable of a word (including monosyllabic words) often mutate to a different vowel when a suffix is added. Examples of such mutations are as follows:{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=30–1, 42}}
* {{IPA|/ai/}} > {{IPA|/əi~ɛi/}}; e.g. {{lang|cy|dail}} 'foliage', {{lang|cy|deilen}} 'leaf'
* {{IPA|/aɨ/}} > {{IPA|/əɨ~ɛɨ/}}; e.g. {{lang|cy|haul}} 'sun', {{lang|cy|heulog}} 'sunny'
* {{IPA|/ai/}} > {{IPA|/a/}}; e.g. {{lang|cy|gwraig}} 'wife', {{lang|cy|gwragedd}} 'wives'
* {{IPA|/əɨ~ɛɨ/}} > {{IPA|/a/}}; e.g. {{lang|cy|lleidr}} 'thief', {{lang|cy|lladron}} 'thieves'
* {{IPA|/au/}} > {{IPA|/ɔ/}}: e.g. {{lang|cy|tlawd}} 'poor' {{lang|cy|tlotaf}} 'poorest'; {{lang|cy|awr}} 'hour', {{lang|cy|oriau}} 'hours'; 
* {{IPA|/ɨ/}} > {{IPA|/ə/}}; {{lang|cy|byr}} {{IPA|/bɨr/}} 'short', {{lang|cy|byrrach}} {{IPA|/ˈbəraχ/}} 'shorter' 
* {{IPA|/u/}} > {{IPA|/ə/}}; {{lang|cy|trwm}} {{IPA|/trum/}} 'heavy', {{lang|cy|trymach}} {{IPA|/ˈtrəmaχ/}} 'heavier'; {{lang|cy|cwch}} {{IPA|/kuːχ/}} 'boat', {{lang|cy|cychod}} {{IPA|/'kəχod/}} 'boats' 
* {{IPA|/ɨuχ/}} > {{IPA|/ɨχ/}}, e.g. {{lang|cy|uwch}} {{IPA|/ɨuχ/}} 'higher', {{lang|cy|uchaf}} {{IPA|/ˈɨχav/}} 'highest' 

The change from {{IPA|/ɨ/}} to {{IPA|/ə/}} only occurs when {{IPA|/ɨ/}} is spelled ''y''; when {{IPA|/ɨ/}} in a final syllable is spelled ''u'', there is no change. Thus the vowel changes in {{lang|cy|dydd}} {{IPA|/dɨːð/}} 'day', {{lang|cy|dyddiau}} {{IPA|/ˈdəðje/}} 'days', but not in {{lang|cy|llun}} {{IPA|/ɬɨːn/}} 'picture', {{lang|cy|lluniau}} {{IPA|/ˈɬɨnje/}} 'pictures'. In some words the penultimate vowel also changes, e.g. {{lang|cy|cwmwl}} {{IPA|/ˈkumul/}} 'cloud', {{lang|cy|cymylau}} {{IPA|/kəˈməle/}} 'clouds'.

It is thought that these vowel mutations may have arisen at a period when the stress in Welsh was still on the final syllable of two-syllable words; when the stress shifted to the penultimate syllable, the reduced vowels remained even though they were now in stressed syllables.{{sfnp|Williams|1983|pp=242–3}} The mutation {{IPA|/au/}} > {{IPA|/ɔ/}} also occurs in final syllables (e.g. {{lang|cy|dwylo}} 'hands' from {{lang|cy|llaw}} 'hand'), but this is thought to have arisen after the stress moved from the final to the penultimate syllable.{{sfnp|Willis|2009}}

====Penultimate i-affection====
A different set of changes, known as penultimate i-affection, takes place when the suffix begins with {{IPA|/i, ɨ/}} or {{IPA|/j/}}. These mostly concern the vowel {{IPA|/a/}} or diphthongs beginning with {{IPA|/a/}}:{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=35, 41–2}}

Before {{IPA|/i/}}:
* {{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/e/}}: e.g. {{lang|cy|truan}} 'wretch(ed)', {{lang|cy|trueni}} 'wretchedness'
* {{IPA|/aːɨ̯/}} > {{IPA|/əi~ɛi/}}: e.g.  {{lang|cy|gwaedd}} 'shout', {{lang|cy|gweiddi}} '(to) shout'
* {{IPA|/au/}} > {{IPA|/əu~ɛu/}}: e.g. {{lang|cy|cawr}} 'giant', {{lang|cy|cewri}} 'giants'

Before {{IPA|/ɨ/}}: 
* {{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/e/}}: e.g.  {{lang|cy|plant}} 'children', {{lang|cy|plentyn}} 'child'
* {{IPA|/aːɨ/}} > {{IPA|/əɨ~ɛɨ/}}: e.g. {{lang|cy|caer}} 'fortified town', {{lang|cy|ceyrydd}} 'fortified towns'; {{lang|cy|aeth}} '(s)he went', {{lang|cy|euthum}} 'I went'
* {{IPA|/aːu/}} > {{IPA|/əu~ɛu/}}: e.g. {{lang|cy|taw}} 'silence', {{lang|cy|tewych}} 'silence'

Before {{IPA|/j/}}:
*{{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/əi~ɛi/}}: {{lang|cy|mab}} 'son', {{lang|cy|meibion}} 'sons'
*{{IPA|/e/}} > {{IPA|/əi~ɛi/}}: {{lang|cy|gorwedd}} 'lie down', {{lang|cy|gorweiddiog}} 'lying down'
*{{IPA|/aːɨ̯/}} > {{IPA|/əi~ɛi/}}: {{lang|cy|draen}} 'thorn', {{lang|cy|dreiniog}} 'thorny'

====Ultimate i-affection====
In some cases i-affection takes place in a final syllable or in a monosyllable where there was formerly an i-suffix which is no longer pronounced. This kind of i-affection is frequent in plural nouns and is also found in some adjectives and some 3rd person singular verbs. The changes, however, differ from those shown above where a suffix is still present. In a two-syllable word if the penultimate vowel is {{IPA|/a/}}, as in {{lang|cy|dafad}} or {{lang|cy|alarch}}, it changes to {{IPA|/e/}} by penultimate i-affection:{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=34, 39}}

*{{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/ai/}}: {{lang|cy|dafad}} 'sheep (sg.)', {{lang|cy|defaid}} 'sheep (pl.)'
*{{IPA|/aː/}} > {{IPA|/ai/}}: {{lang|cy|brân}} 'crow', {{lang|cy|brain}} 'crows'
*{{IPA|/aːɨ/}} > {{IPA|/ai/}}: {{lang|cy|draen}} 'thorn', {{lang|cy|drain}} 'thorns'
*{{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/əi~ɛi/}}: {{lang|cy|car}} 'car', {{lang|cy|ceir}} 'cars'; {{lang|cy|arth}} 'bear', {{lang|cy|eirth}} 'bears'
*{{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/ɨ/}}: {{lang|cy|alarch}} 'swan', {{lang|cy|elyrch}} 'swans'
*{{IPA|/ɔ/}} > {{IPA|/ɨ/}}: {{lang|cy|ffordd}} 'road', {{lang|cy|ffyrdd}} 'roads'
*{{IPA|/ʊ/}} > {{IPA|/ɨ/}}: {{lang|cy|asgwrn}} 'bone', {{lang|cy|esgyrn}} 'bones'; {{lang|cy|gŵr}} 'husband', {{lang|cy|gwŷr}} 'husbands'
*{{IPA|/ɔɨ/}} > {{IPA|/ʊɨ/}}: {{lang|cy|oen}} 'lamb', {{lang|cy|ŵyn}} 'lambs'

Examples in plural adjectives are:{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|pp=66–7}}
*{{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/ai/}}: {{lang|cy|bychan}} > {{lang|cy|bychain}} 'small' 
*{{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/əi~ɛi/}}: {{lang|cy|hardd}} > {{lang|cy|heirdd}} 'handsome'; {{lang|cy|marw}} > {{lang|cy|meirw}} 'dead'

An example in a verb is:
*{{IPA|/ɔ/}} > {{IPA|/ɨ/}}: {{lang|cy|agor}} 'open', {{lang|cy|egyr}} '(s)he opens (will open)'

====Ultimate a-affection====
Another vowel change, concerning only the vowels {{IPA|/ɨ, ɨː/}} (when spelled ''y'') and {{IPA|/ʊ, uː/}}, is found in the feminine of adjectives. This change is called a-affection, since it is believed to have arisen from a lost feminine suffix ''-a''. (For example, Brythonic *{{IPA|/birraː/}} > MW. {{lang|cy|berr}} 'short (fem.)', as against *{{IPA|/birros/}} > MW. {{lang|cy|byrr}} 'short (masc.)'.){{sfnp|Willis|2009}} Many of these adjectives are single-syllable words:{{sfnp|Morris-Jones|1921|p=33}}

*{{IPA|/ɨ/}} > {{IPA|/ɛ/}}: {{lang|cy|gwyn}} 'white (m.)', {{lang|cy|gwɛn}} 'white (f.)'; {{lang|cy|trydydd}} 'third (m.)', {{lang|cy|trydedd}} 'third (f.)'
*{{IPA|/ɨː/}} > {{IPA|/eː/}}: {{lang|cy|cryf}} 'strong (m.)', {{lang|cy|cref}} 'strong (f.)'
*{{IPA|/ʊ/}} > {{IPA|/ɔ/}}: {{lang|cy|trwm}} 'heavy (m.)', {{lang|cy|trom}} 'heavy (f.)'; {{lang|cy|hwn}} 'this (m.)', {{lang|cy|hon}} 'this (f.)'
*{{IPA|/uː/}} > {{IPA|/oː/}}: {{lang|cy|tlws}} 'pretty (m.)', {{lang|cy|tlos}} 'pretty (f.)'

The affected vowel is usually in the ultimate (or only) syllable, but occasionally the penultimate is affected, as in the following word:
*{{IPA|/ə/}} > {{IPA|/ɛ/}}: {{lang|cy|bychan}} 'small (m.)', {{lang|cy|bechan}} 'small (f.)'

In the following word, both vowels are changed, with a-affection of {{IPA|/ɨ/}} > {{IPA|/ɛ/}} in the final syllable of the feminine, and i-affection of {{IPA|/a/}} > {{IPA|/e/}} in the penultimate syllable of the masculine form:
*{{IPA|/ɨ/}} > {{IPA|/ɛ/}}:  {{lang|cy|pedwerydd}} 'fourth (m.)', {{lang|cy|pedwaredd}} 'fourth (f.)'

After a noun, the feminine adjective has soft mutation of the first letter, e.g. {{lang|cy|rhaff gref}} 'a strong rope', {{lang|cy|cath wen}} 'a white cat'; but the masculine adjective is used after {{lang|cy|a}} 'and', e.g. {{lang|cy|cath ddu a gwyn}} 'a black-and-white cat'. Feminine adjectives are only used with singular nouns, as there is no distinction of gender in the plural in Welsh.

==Stress and pitch==
[[Stress (linguistics)|Stress]] falls in the vast majority of polysyllabic words on the [[wikt:penultimate|penultimate]] [[syllable]]. There are three main sources of exception.{{sfnp|Griffiths|Jones|1995|at=Quantity of vowels: G. Stress and length of vowels in polysyllables}} First, in a few native words, the stress falls on the final syllable (e.g. verbs ending in -áu and words like {{lang|cy|Cymraeg}} 'Welsh') as a result of a stressed penultimate syllable [[Fusion (phonetics)|coalescing]] with a following vowel to form a diphthong or long monophthong. Second, certain prefixes do not reliably take stress (e.g., {{lang|cy|di-}} 'without', as in {{lang|cy|diwerth}} 'worthless', which is stressed on the final syllable). Third, borrowings from other languages often retain the stress in the original language, as with {{lang|cy|ambiwlans}} and {{lang|cy|testament}} (both stressed on the first syllable), though even here stress generally shifts to the penultimate in inflected forms such as the plural.{{sfnp|Williams|1983|p=?}}{{sfnp|Hannahs|2013|pp=44–47}} When syllables are added to the end of a word the stress moves correspondingly:

